# One line per example. Fields are separated by `|` and are, in order:
#
#   name      the directory under the repository root
#   needs     what the run requires, comma-separated; `-` for nothing beyond
#             a POSIX shell. Recognised: cargo curl dotnet java javac mvn node
#             npm python3 tsc cc c++ cmake make maturin, plus
#               zerodds-src        a zero-dds checkout at $ZERODDS_SRC
#               zerodds-cdylib     $ZERODDS_LIB, the built C ABI shared library
#               zerodds-jar        the OMG DDS Java binding jar
#               maturin-installed  `import zerodds` works
#               ts-node-build      crates/ts-node built
#               ts-wasm-build      crates/ts-wasm built
#               pki-files          $ZERODDS_PKI holds the six DDS-Security files
#               rti-connext        an RTI Connext install with a runtime licence
#                                  at $NDDSHOME. Not `peer:rti`: the example's
#                                  own run.sh generates, builds and starts the
#                                  RTI peer, so the toolchain is what has to be
#                                  present and that is answerable.
#               cyclone-py         `import cyclonedds` works (the Cyclone DDS
#                                  Python binding)
#               cyclone-c          idlc and the C library under $CYCLONE
#               fastdds            fastddsgen on PATH, Fast DDS 3 under
#                                  $FASTDDS_HOME
#               opendds            a built OpenDDS tree at $DDS_ROOT
#               opensplice         OpenSplice CE 6.9 at $OSPL_HOME (idlpp and
#                                  release.com)
#               ros2-rmw-zerodds   a ROS 2 setup.bash at $ROS_SETUP and
#                                  rmw_zerodds_cpp installed under $RMW_PREFIX
#               linux              a Linux kernel (sched_setscheduler and friends)
#               peer:<impl>        a second implementation on the wire
#               multicast          a real interface, not loopback
#   verdict   run | build | blocked
#               run      the command runs and its output is checked
#               build    there is no program to run; `note` says why
#               blocked  a named defect stops it; `note` names the defect
#   cmd       executed with `eval` from the example's own directory; `-` when
#             the verdict is `build`. A `blocked` row carries the command the
#             defect stops: the harness runs it and fails the row either with
#             its actual error or a stale-blocker diagnostic on success. The
#             historical note alone cannot identify the current failure.
#   expect    a fixed string the stdout must contain; `-` for none
#   note      free text; required for `build` and `blocked`
#
# A missing tool is never a pass and never a silent skip: it is named and the
# example is counted as unverified by that run.

